Relicensing record (decided 2026-10-12)
---------------------------------------

Some modules of this plugin were adapted from 3LC's sibling plugin
3lc-compute-plugin-kaggle (https://github.com/3lc-ai/3lc-compute-plugin-kaggle),
whose repository is distributed under the GNU Affero General Public License v3.
3LC Inc. is the sole copyright holder of that code and, as the copyright holder,
ships the adapted modules in this project under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
Each adapted file carries this header, verbatim:

    # Adapted from 3lc-compute-plugin-kaggle (Copyright 3LC AI), relicensed by the copyright holder
    # under Apache-2.0 for this project.

Decision: recorded by Rishikesh Jadhav (3LC) on 2026-10-12. Gudbrand Tandberg (3LC)
agreed with the approach. Paul is out of office and will be informed; the
decision is reversible if he objects. The list of adapted files is kept in
docs/STUDY.md section G-5 and is enforced by tests/test_packaging.py.

Evidence, checked on 2026-10-12 against a fresh clone of the origin repository
(head 22183ad, tag v1.2.15, all 5 remote branches and 20 tags):

- Authorship. The origin repository's whole history, 213 commits across every
  ref (first commit 2026-07-20, last 2026-09-14), has one author and committer,
  Rishikesh Jadhav, a 3LC employee; five commits were committed through the
  GitHub web interface by the same author. No commit carries a Co-authored-by,
  Signed-off-by or Reviewed-by trailer. Per file, with renames followed, every
  origin file an adapted module derives from has that single author:
  config_store.py (11 commits), downloader.py (6), predictor.py (20),
  trainer.py (16), ui.html (73), routes.py (28), tests/test_url_regex_parity.py (1),
  scripts/make_kit_manifest.py (1), tests/conftest.py (3), tests/test_config_store.py (1),
  tests/test_downloader.py (5), tests/test_packaging.py (2), tests/test_jobs_bridge.py (1),
  tests/test_ui_node_lifetime.py (1). No outside author appears anywhere.
- No Ultralytics code. The origin modules that mention Ultralytics or YOLO do so
  only in parts that were not carried: predictor.py's run_inference (the YOLO
  call) and the device docstring, trainer.py's training call, checkpoint
  constants and tlc_ultralytics imports, ui.html's licence band and YOLO format
  copy, and conftest.py's from_yolo_url stub. The carried parts (the session
  store, the downloader's stage machine, the kaggle client functions, the
  kaggle live-status shape, the bounds message and the run-parameter readback
  shapes, the fragment's shell and tab structure, the test patterns) contain
  none, and this project's own tree imports nothing from Ultralytics; its only
  mentions are two comments and a test asserting that the Ultralytics band and
  the YOLO copy are absent. Training, inference and the data pipeline here are
  written against timm, torch, torchvision and tlc.
- The adapted files in this project: src/kaggle_classification/kaggle_client.py,
  kit.py, predictor.py, session.py, status.py, trainer.py, ui/ui.html;
  tests/conftest.py, test_ctx_adapter.py, test_kit.py, test_packaging.py,
  test_session.py, test_ui_node_lifetime.py, test_url_regex_parity.py;
  tools/build_kit.py.

Code copied from 3lc-compute-plugin-timm (scripts/release_version.py,
tests/test_release_version.py) was already Apache-2.0 and keeps its origin header.
